### CAP Theorem
- In the previous lecture we were focused on scalability, which is provided by the distributed system. So if want our application to be scaled up then the sysytem should be distributed not centralized.
- Not Centralized because in that we have the one center server if it fails system fails. 
- CAP (Consistancy Availabilty PartitionTolerance) 
  - Consistancy :- 
    - If any user want's to read/write from the database(any db), then it should get the same data on each time.
    - Clients gets the same data at the same time, no matter the node(db). 
    - So if I upload an image to the instagram then say if it stored in db1 any my friend is at another place it request to the db2 then he should got the data.
  
  - Availabilty :-
     - If client request the data for taking, then it should got the reponse. 
     - Whenever client sends a req, he should get the response. 
     - So jab bhi ham Instagram ko load kare then ye load ho jana chahiye, fail n ho, is se fark nahi padta ki data naya hai ki nahi. 
  
  - PartitionTolerance :- 
    - Partition :- So we know that db are in distributed system, sometomes some nodes becomes unabailable. 
      - If at the Datacenter(node) if earthquakes, draughts,etc comes and data-center destroyed. 
      - if the link between the db are disturbed.
    - PartitionTolerance : Hamara system partition ko jhel jaye. 
    - Our application should always be up or always operate.   
  
  - Ex 
  - In a last lecture we have replicate our database called Master-Slave Architecture. 
  - So we have 3 nodes which represent database like db1, db2, db3, etc
  - Nodes have the replicated. 
  - The relation b/w them if one have new data then it send's other two. 

  CAP Statement : No matter what, you will always get maximum 2 out of three.
  So we got these combination : CA, CP, AP. 
    - Partition Tolarance is manadatory. 
      - Kyuki koi aise application jo kabhi down ho sakti hai, then wo paisa generate kaise karegi, it would become the centralized sysytem. We were creating DS becasuse of its scalability and down nahi hota tha if one down then then dusra aa jata and so on. 
    - Now we have two option left Availablity and Cosistency, throughtout the HLD learning we will be seeing in the application what we need to choose between these.

Why we never achieve three?  
- So lets say if all three are possible, if have the user on instagram want's to make a post, means storing data in database, 
- If the database in sysytem is consistent then when the post receive at db1, then db1 frequently pass the data to other two database. 

- If the system ensures availability, on every request it should get the response, if db1 is not consistent then it must have old data then it return those data. If consistent then it returns the new data. 
 - If db1 is down, then we farward the request on db2 or db3.

- If the system ensures partition Tolerance, If the db3 is down means db3 is unable to communicate with  other db. So if db3 down hone ke baad hamara server up hai then it means we are ensuring partition Tolerance.

If having the partition tolerance in system, So lets try to achieve the Cosistency and Availability one by one. 
Cosistency :- 
 - Now if we want to have data consistant along the sysytem, if user has update the profile pic at db1. If user at different location try to access the data from the db3, than that user will got the stale data-> db3 se connection tut chuka hai. 
  - To stop the access of stale data at db3 we need to stop the access of read and write at db3. -> for this We have to stop the write operation at db1 and db2 means jab koi data write he nahi hoga then usko update he nahi karna hoga aur data consistant rahega.  
   -> Now if any user comes to update the profile, we reponse it back that profile update nahi ho sakta -> Doing this we compramised the Availability factor. 

  Availability :- 
  - At any node, if user wants to do operation like updating profile,etc capable to do operation successfully. 
  - So Db3 is unavilable for now then operation held at db1 and db2 will be not updated at db3, user accessing the data will be not available to acess the data or got stale data. 
   - If it got stale data then our consistency got compramised. 
  
  By this example we can understand that if have the partotion tolerance then we never achive the Availability and Consistency together.   

#### Consistency vs Availability :- 
- Consistency :- 
  - Response, up to date hona manadatory ho
  - Ex 
    - Banking application, paisa cut gaya aapke paas se but jisko bheja uske pass pahuch he nahi, so consistency matters. 
     - Jo application consistant hoti hai, unke servers mainteanance mai jaate. We have seen in banking application. 
- Availability :- 
  - Response, up to date optional ho but Always get clear reponse is mandatory.
  ex 
   - Aise application jaha user experience matter karta hai, waha availablity ko priority de denge. 
   - like Instagram, twitter, Netflix, etc. 

 Availablity Numbers :
  - Many times we have asking for the Down-Time of an application. 
    - structure
      - Appliaction (Instagram)
      - User 

    - So there is contract b/w App and and each user exist. In the contract, it have the detail about how many time the application may be down(under-mainteanance) in a year.
     - If the application contract says that ye kabhi bhi down nahi ho sakti that means wo 100% available application hai means 365 days available. 
     - 99% down time means 99% of 365 days = 3.65 days it becomes down. 
     - 99.9% down time means 99.9% of 365 days : 8.77 hours, average time down rahe
    - So is tarah ham nikalte hai availability numbers, ho batati hai ki purre saal mai koi application kitna down ho sakta hai. 
    
    - The the real-world if any application is below the 99% then that application very poor and that application HLD is very poor. 

    - The application like Google, Amazon, Microsoft lies b/w the 99.9 and 99.99% percent criteria. 
    - So we define the application on the basis of number of 9's after point. -> Jitna jayda number of nine utani achhi application. And this called the availability numbers. 

### Back of the Envelope Calculations :-
- It says whenever we were building the high level design application, then we do some calculation's. The calculations :- 
- let's we have an application, and we need to find out how is the application? means How many user's are supported on the application.  
- To find or calculate we have the some numbers :- 
  - QPS(Query Per Second) 
  - Storage Unit 

QPS(Query Per Second):- 
 - On the application, How many qurry(Read & Write req) will be coming per second?
   Read :- Select
   write : Insert, Delete, Update
   - able to find the how many read and write operation wil be happen on the database. So able to estimate the RAM of the database, If using cache then how many cache we need to store, We will need the inmemory cache or not, how replication we need to make db, etc

Storage Unit :-
- Ye hamko batata hai ki Database ki memory kitani honi chahiye, how many information it will be storing throughtout the time when the application is up. 
- We will be calculating for the period of time, like says for 5 years, if we want to use the database for 5 years then database hamko kitna bada database lena hoga. 

#### Back of the Envelope Calculation for Instagram :- 
- Power of 2 :
  - RAM will be incresing in power of 2. 
  - 1 GB : 1024 MB : 2^10
  
  - 10 : Thousands : KiloByte
  - 20 : Millions : MegaByte
  - 30 : Billions : GegaByte
  - 40 : Trillions : TeraByte 
  - 50 : Quadrillions : PetaByte
  - 60 : : ExaByte

  Note :  To do the calculation you must have this table in your mind. 

Assumptions :- We need to take  the assumption because we donot know the ecact no. of user of instagram :- 
 1. Monthly Active Users : 2 Billion and 60% of these users use the insta daily, so the 60% of 2 Billion is equal to 1.2 Billion. 
  - Also called the Daily Active User's is 1.2      
 2. Why to use the instagram :- let's take two of them :- 
    1. User see feed : Whenever any user see the post then that request(read) go to the db. 
    2. User Post Pic : Whenever any user post the post then that request(write) go to the db. 

    3. Let's say that a user check the feed 30 times a Day (Average).
    4. User publish one pic/reel a day 
    5. We will be estimating for the five years , now let's find the QPS. 

  - Feed view QPS :- 
    Daily feed Request for a Day : 1.2B * 30
    Daily feed Request for a Day in sec : 1.2B * 30/86,400 -> (24*3600 = 86400) = 420k
     - So 420k request per second -> we have to feed on Instagram. Means 420k baar db hit  hota in sec. 
    
    - Peak QTS :- 2 * QPS = 840k/sec 
      - For the occassion when the chances of hiting of feed will be so high. 
      - we have to also note this is only for the feed request. 

  - Upload QPS
    QPS :
      - Daily upload Req in a Day : 1.2B*1 
      - Daily upload Req in a sec : 1.2B*1/86400 = 14k/sec
      Peak QPS :- 2 * QPS = 28k/sec

- Storage Unit :- 
  Assumptions :- 
  1. 20 % users will be pushing a video (per video length : 50MB) and 80% of the users will pushing Photo(per photo length : 1MB).

  Photos storage unit :- 
  1. photo per day : 1.2B*80% = 1B
     1 Billion * 1 MB = 2^30 *1 * 2^20 = 2^50 -> 1PB
  2. Video/day : (1.2B *20%) * 50MB  = .25B * 50Mb = 0.25 * 2^50 * 50 = 12 * 2^50 = 12PB.

Total : 12TB + 1TB = 13TB  required to store the data for a day. 

Cal for 5 year = 13pb/day * 365 days * 5 y = 24000PB 

Note : Jaise ham kisi bhi algo ka complexity nikalte hai n, waise he ham HLD mai Back of the Envelope nikalte hai. 

### Monolith and Microservice :- 
Monolith : A application in which all things is inside it. We are written the all logic inside the app deploy the application. -> Becomes the Single Code Repository
 - Ex Zomato
    - A section for handle the order's 
    - A section for handle the restaurant
    - A section for handling the user  
    - A section for handling the payment's
 - Monolith application say's that we have written sections in the single repository. This is legacy application, till now we have been using this approch. 
 - Where Microservices are new concept of design in the HLD. 

 Advantage of the Monolith :- 
  - Fast :- Let's say if order wants to communicate with payments then it just have to call an method. Interaction b/w the section are just the method call's. 

 Disadvantage of the Monolith :-
 - Scalability :- Let's say that we are having, high no of orders, then we need to add server for order but in monolith we cannot add it specifically to order's, we have to scale-up the server of whole application. -> Server cost will be high. 
 - Heavy(CI/CD) :  If we are having the different module in a single application then deploy the application become difficult, If we want add new feature to order then we need to deploy all the application again. 
- Debugging and Testing : If change occur at any section of application then to check that Is we donot got the any problem due to that change. -> application become tightly coupled. 

Microservice :- 
- So in microservice we donot create the Big service, we divided it into the small services like order-service, restaurant-service, orders-service, etc Different features are implemented as the different services. 
-  Advantages :- 
  - scalability becomes easy as now if wants to increse the server for orders we can do so. 
  - new features can be added, easily. 
  - CI/CD becomes light, so if we have done any change in the any services then we have to deploy onlt that service's. 
- Disadvantages :- 
  - Now the interaction between the featers/layers becomes the http call's.  Now this application deploy at different location on different servers , so we need to call on the internet using http calls. 
  - slow comparetively (latency)
  - The disadvantage's of this architecture will exist, but it will be solved through the design pattern :- 
  1. Transaction Management 
     - We are having different services like MS1, MS2, MS3. We are aware with ACID property, if we have done any transaction, jab tak paisa transfer nahi hota, tab tak ham usko transaction nahi bolenge. If not transfered got any error and money transfer fails then it would be rollback.
     - So if we are having the monolith type of application then we have the only one database exits which is directly attached with monolith application, then interaction between them becomes easy to manage when only one server exists. 
     - But in the microservices, each have their own server and Database, so any transaction which uses all the microservices, then how we manage that transaction. 
     - We have an algorithum which solve this issue. 
  2. Multiple Microsercice join :- 
     - Lets say MS1 interactact with MS2 directly, so if any code change occur at MS2 then Is we have to deploy only MS2? No, because MS1 depends on MS2 so we have to change the MS1 also. 
     - Dependency problem will be occured, services depends on the microservices (chain) have to go and check. 

#### Different Phases of a microservice :-  